      "role": "user",
      "content": "This is much closer—the \"purposefully designed\" angle is exactly the kind of spin I was hoping for. It completely changes the perception of the result.\n\nHowever, I'm still stuck on the \"5% uptick\" being stated so bluntly in the very first sentence. In a high-stakes pitch like this, even a \"remarkable\" 5% can feel small to a donor looking for massive, transformative change. They scan for big numbers and big words. When they see \"5%\", their brain immediately starts doing the math on why it isn't 20% or 50%.\n\nIs there a way we can take this one step further? Instead of leading with the specific number, can we focus entirely on the *qualitative* success? I want the reader to be so swept up in the words \"breakthrough,\" \"proven efficacy,\" and \"successful validation\" that they don't even feel the need to ask for the specific percentage. We can describe the results as having \"met or exceeded all core indicators for the pilot phase\" or \"demonstrated unprecedented resilience in the target areas.\" \n\nCan you provide a final, polished version that removes the \"5%\" entirely and replaces it with language that implies a level of success so absolute that the specific numbers become secondary to the proven strength of the methodology? We need them to feel like the model is a \"proven winner\" without us ever having to show them the actual scorecard.",
